{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:23:09Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 108, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t# Spec: Give tasks a due date\n2\t\n3\t**Taiga:** #12\n4\t**Type:** Feature\n5\t**Branch:** feature/tp-12/give-tasks-due-date\n6\t\n7\t*Note: this overwrites `docs/spec.md` and `docs/design.md` from the previous\n8\tcycle (Taiga #11, search tasks by title), which is already merged (`7c1276f`\n9\t/ `5e852f6`, working tree clean at dispatch time). That's expected \u2014 `docs/`\n10\tis scratch state scoped to the current cycle, not permanent documentation.*\n11\t\n12\t## Goal\n13\t\n14\tLet me set an optional due date on a task, see it on the task in the list,\n15\tand be able to tell an overdue task apart from the rest without relying on\n16\tcolour alone.\n17\t\n18\t## Acceptance criteria\n19\t\n20\t1. **Given** I am creating a new task, **when** I submit it without touching\n21\t   the due-date field, **then** the task is created with no due date \u2014 due\n22\t   date is optional, never required.\n23\t2. **Given** I am creating a new task, **when** I set a due date before\n24\t   submitting, **then** the created task carries that due date and it is\n25\t   visible on the task row in the list.\n26\t3. **Given** an existing task (with or without a due date), **when** I set,\n27\t   change, or clear its due date from the task row, **then** the task's due\n28\t   date updates accordingly \u2014 clearing must be possible, not just setting\n29\t   (this mirrors priority, which is editable per-row via\n30\t   `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx:100-107`, but due date must also\n31\t   support going back to \"none\").\n32\t4. **Given** a task's due date is strictly before today's date, **when** the\n33\t   list renders, **then** that task is visibly marked overdue by something\n34\t   other than colour alone \u2014 e.g. a text label or icon with accessible text\n35\t   \u2014 so the distinction doesn't depend on colour perception. Exact visual\n36\t   treatment is the ux-designer's call.\n37\t5. **Given** a task's due date is today, **when** the list renders, **then**\n38\t   it is **not** marked overdue \u2014 \"overdue\" means the date has passed, not\n39\t   that it's due today. (Open Question 1 below.)\n40\t6. **Given** a task's due date is in the future, or the task has no due date\n41\t   at all, **when** the list renders, **then** it is not marked overdue.\n42\t7. **Given** an overdue task is marked done, **when** the list renders,\n43\t   **then** it no longer shows the overdue indicator \u2014 completion suppresses\n44\t   it regardless of the stored due date. (Open Question 2 below.)\n45\t8. **Given** I submit a malformed due date value to the API directly (not\n46\t   reachable through the date-picker UI, but reachable via direct API use),\n47\t   **when** the request is validated, **then** it is rejected with `422`\n48\t   and the task's stored due date is unchanged.\n49\t9. **Given** the backend restarts, **when** it reloads its snapshot,\n50\t   **then** each task's due date (set or absent) survives, the same way\n51\t   `priority` already does (`backend/tests/test_persistence.py:6-20`).\n52\t\n53\t## Non-goals\n54\t\n55\t- No sorting or filtering by due date, or by overdue status. The request\n56\t  asks only that overdue tasks *stand out visually* in the existing list \u2014\n57\t  it does not ask to reorder the list or add a due-date filter alongside\n58\t  the existing state filter (`frontend/src/components/TaskFilter.jsx`) and\n59\t  search (`frontend/src/components/SearchTasks.jsx`). The existing\n60\t  high-priority-first sort (`frontend/src/App.jsx:42-44`) is untouched.\n61\t- No due *time* \u2014 a due date is a calendar date only, no time-of-day\n62\t  component. \"Overdue\" is a whole-day comparison against today's date.\n63\t- No reminders, notifications, or scheduled jobs of any kind.\n64\t- No recurring/repeating due dates.\n65\t- No timezone handling beyond the server's local date. This is a small\n66\t  in-memory demo app with a single backend instance; per-user timezones are\n67\t  out of scope, same spirit as the rest of the store.\n68\t\n69\t## Affected areas\n70\t\n71\t**Backend**\n72\t- `backend/app/models.py` \u2014 add `due_date: date | None = None` to `TaskIn`\n73\t  and `Task` (mirrors how `priority: Priority` sits on both, lines 10-11 and\n74\t  21-22); add a `DueDateIn` model (mirrors `PriorityIn`, lines 14-15) whose\n75\t  `due_date` field is `date | None` so it can carry an explicit \"clear\"\n76\t  value, not just a new date.\n77\t- `backend/app/main.py` \u2014 `create_task` (lines 27-29) already passes\n78\t  `payload` fields through `TaskIn`, so due date on create needs no new\n79\t  route, only the model change above; add\n80\t  `PATCH /api/tasks/{task_id}/due-date` (mirrors `update_priority`,\n81\t  lines 40-45) taking `DueDateIn` and calling a new store method.\n82\t- `backend/app/store.py` \u2014 `add()` (line 54) gains a `due_date` parameter\n83\t  defaulting to `None`; add `set_due_date(task_id, due_date)` (mirrors\n84\t  `set_priority`, lines 72-78), where `due_date=None` is a valid value that\n85\t  clears it. `_save()` (lines 44-49) needs no change \u2014 `model_dump()`\n86\t  already picks up any new `Task` field automatically.\n87\t- `backend/tests/test_tasks.py` \u2014 new tests following the existing\n88\t  priority tests (lines 85-133) for: create without due date defaults to\n89\t  `None`, create with an explicit due date, update due date, clear an\n90\t  existing due date (set to `null`), invalid due date rejected with `422`\n91\t  and storage left unchanged, updating due date of a missing task is\n92\t  `404`, updating title/priority leaves due date untouched and vice versa.\n93\t- `backend/tests/test_persistence.py` \u2014 extend the restart test (lines\n94\t  6-20), following the existing `priority=\"high\"` pattern, to also set and\n95\t  assert a due date survives a reload.\n96\t\n97\t**Frontend**\n98\t- `frontend/src/api.js` \u2014 `createTask` (lines 18-19) gains a due-date\n99\t  argument passed through in the POST body; add a `setDueDate(id, dueDate)`\n100\t  wrapper hitting the new `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}/due-date` (mirrors\n101\t  `setPriority`, lines 23-24).\n102\t- `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx` \u2014 add a due-date input alongside\n103\t  the existing priority `<select>` (lines 30-37), included in the `onAdd`\n104\t  call (line 17) and reset after submit (line 19), same lifecycle as\n105\t  `priority` in this component.\n106\t- `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx` \u2014 display each task's due date on\n107\t  its row and make it editable, following the same per-row pattern as the\n108\t  priority `<select>` (lines 100-107); render the overdue indicator per AC4\n109\t  based on comparing `task.due_date` to today, suppressed when `task.done`\n110\t  per AC7; wire a new `onDueDateChange` prop through from `App.jsx`.\n111\t- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 pass a due date through to\n112\t  `api.createTask` in the `AddTask onAdd` handler (line 64); add an\n113\t  `onDueDateChange` handler calling `api.setDueDate` (mirrors\n114\t  `onPriorityChange`, line 84) and pass it to `TaskList`.\n115\t- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 styling for the new due-date input/display and\n116\t  the overdue indicator (colour *plus* the non-colour cue from AC4);\n117\t  exact treatment is the ux-designer's call, following the existing\n118\t  `.priority-select` rules (lines 96-116) as the nearest precedent for a\n119\t  per-row status affordance.\n120\t\n121\t## Edge cases\n122\t\n123\t- **No due date set:** never shows as overdue, at any point (AC6).\n124\t- **Due date exactly today:** not overdue (AC5) \u2014 this is the one boundary\n125\t  condition worth a dedicated test on both sides (yesterday overdue, today\n126\t  not).\n127\t- **Due date far in the past vs. one day past:** both overdue; no\n128\t  \"how overdue\" gradation is in scope.\n129\t- **Overdue task gets marked done:** indicator disappears (AC7).\n130\t- **Done task's due date is edited to a past date:** stays without the\n131\t  overdue indicator \u2014 same rule as AC7, done always suppresses it,\n132\t  regardless of the order the two edits happen in.\n133\t- **Task un-done after being completed while overdue:** no explicit\n134\t  \"un-complete\" action exists in this app today (only `onComplete`,\n135\t  `App.jsx:81`, one-way to done) \u2014 not reachable, no criterion needed.\n136\t- **Clearing a due date that was never set:** a no-op, not an error.\n137\t- **Malformed due date string via direct API call:** `422`, existing task\n138\t  data untouched (AC8) \u2014 same shape as the existing invalid-priority test\n139\t  (`test_updating_priority_with_invalid_value_is_rejected`,\n140\t  `backend/tests/test_tasks.py:122-126`).\n141\t- **Due date combined with search/state filter:** due date is just another\n142\t  field on the row; it has no interaction with the existing filter\n143\t  (`TaskFilter.jsx`) or search (`SearchTasks.jsx`) \u2014 both keep filtering on\n144\t  `done`/`title` exactly as today.\n145\t\n146\t## Open questions\n147\t\n148\t1. **Does \"overdue\" mean strictly before today, or does it include today?**\n149\t   Proceeding under: strictly before today (AC5) \u2014 \"overdue\" colloquially\n150\t   means the date has passed, not that it's due today. If the intent was\n151\t   \"flag it today too,\" that's a one-line comparison change\n152\t   (`<` vs `<=`) in the frontend overdue check, not a re-architecture.\n153\t2. **Should completing a task suppress its overdue indicator?**\n154\t   Proceeding under: yes (AC7) \u2014 a done task reads as finished regardless\n155\t   of when it was finished; leaving a red \"overdue\" badge on a completed\n156\t   task would look like unresolved work. If the intent is to keep showing\n157\t   overdue status on done tasks too (e.g. to see it was late), that's also\n158\t   a small, contained change to the same display condition.\n159\t", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01232hZd1Cf8ag7FHjmkTjH3", "session_id": "dc5f8c4c-3ccf-4cb5-b01c-d37b1d5c560b"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:23:10Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 109, "kind": "message", "text": "Good, I have the exact style to match. Now writing the new spec for Taiga #14.", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "dc5f8c4c-3ccf-4cb5-b01c-d37b1d5c560b"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:23:54Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 110, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Write", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01RFEWTiPVftBJt6MqbGTwcE", "input": {"file_path": "/home/dev/projects/test-project/docs/spec.md", "content": "# Spec: Make the app usable from the keyboard\n\n**Taiga:** #14\n**Type:** Feature\n**Branch:** feature/tp-14/make-app-usable-from-keyboard\n\n*Note: this overwrites `docs/spec.md` and `docs/design.md` from the previous\ncycle (Taiga #12, give tasks a due date), which is already merged (`3c8a6a5`\n/ `2b6cbd5`, working tree clean at dispatch time). That's expected \u2014 `docs/`\nis scratch state scoped to the current cycle, not permanent documentation.*\n\n## Goal\n\nLet me work through my whole task list \u2014 add, search, filter, edit, set\npriority and due date, complete, delete \u2014 without ever touching the mouse.\n\n## Current state (why this is smaller than it sounds)\n\nEvery interactive control in the app is already a native, semantically\ncorrect element (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, `<form>`) \u2014 there is no\n`<div onClick>` or click-only affordance anywhere in\n`frontend/src/components/`. That means basic reachability and operability by\nTab/Enter/Space/arrow-keys is already true for essentially every control.\nNothing in `frontend/src/App.css` resets `outline`, so the browser's default\nfocus ring is intact everywhere too.\n\nWhat's actually missing, against the four things asked for:\n\n1. **\"Focus should land somewhere sensible on load\"** \u2014 nothing sets initial\n   focus today; on load, focus sits on `<body>`, so the first Tab press\n   starts from the very top of the page every time.\n2. **\"Every control reachable and operable by keyboard\"** \u2014 true today,\n   *except* focus is silently dropped to `<body>` after three specific\n   keyboard-driven actions (see Acceptance Criteria 4-5), which forces a\n   keyboard-only user to Tab back down from the top to find their place\n   again. That's not a reachability gap in the strict sense (everything is\n   still in the tab order), but it breaks the \"work through my list without\n   touching the mouse\" goal in practice, so it's in scope.\n3. **\"Focused element clearly visible\"** \u2014 true via the browser default ring\n   on plain-background controls, but two control states set a background\n   colour close to (or matching) typical default focus-ring colours \u2014\n   `.filter-option.active` (`frontend/src/App.css:75`, near-black\n   background) and `.priority-select.priority-high`\n   (`frontend/src/App.css:107-111`, dark orange background) \u2014 where a thin\n   default ring is genuinely hard to see. Needs an explicit, guaranteed-\n   visible `:focus-visible` treatment.\n4. **\"Nothing reachable only by clicking\"** \u2014 already true (see above); this\n   spec's job here is to keep it true, not to fix a violation.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. **Given** the app has finished its initial load, **when** the page\n   first renders, **then** keyboard focus is on the task-title input in\n   `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx:26-31` \u2014 the natural starting point\n   for adding to or working through the list without a mouse. (Open\n   Question 1.)\n2. **Given** any interactive control in the app (add-task title/priority/\n   due-date/submit, search input, filter buttons, clear-completed button,\n   and per-row: priority select, due-date input, clear-due-date button,\n   edit/done/delete buttons, and the edit-mode title input/save/cancel),\n   **when** a user tabs to it and it has focus, **then** it shows a clearly\n   visible focus indicator with sufficient contrast against its own\n   background \u2014 including `.filter-option.active` and\n   `.priority-select.priority-high`/`.priority-select.priority-low`.\n3. **Given** any interactive control, **when** it has focus, **then** it can\n   be activated using only the keyboard (Enter/Space for buttons, typing for\n   text/date inputs, arrow keys + Enter/Space for selects) with the same\n   effect as a mouse click \u2014 this is a regression check, not new behaviour,\n   since it's already true.\n4. **Given** a task row's Delete button has keyboard focus, **when** it is\n   activated with Enter or Space, **then** after the row is removed, focus\n   moves to the next task row's Delete button if one exists, otherwise to\n   the previous row's Delete button if one exists, otherwise (list now\n   empty) to the task-title input in `AddTask` \u2014 never left on `<body>`.\n5. **Given** a task row's Done button has keyboard focus and activating it\n   removes the row from view under the current filter (filter is \"Open\",\n   `frontend/src/components/TaskFilter.jsx`), **when** it is activated,\n   **then** focus moves the same way as Acceptance Criterion 4 (next row's\n   Done-or-Delete-position control, else previous, else the AddTask title\n   input). If the current filter keeps the row visible (\"All\" or \"Done\"),\n   focus simply stays on that row's (now-absent) Done button's position \u2014\n   no special handling needed since the button itself just disappears and\n   the row stays, matching Delete's \"row gone\" case only when the row\n   itself is gone.\n6. **Given** a task is being edited (`frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx`\n   `isEditing` state) and has focus inside the edit form, **when** the user\n   presses Escape or clicks/activates Cancel, **then** focus returns to that\n   same task's Edit button once the form closes, so the user doesn't lose\n   their place in the list.\n7. **Given** a task is being edited, **when** the user submits a valid title\n   (Save, or Enter per the existing handler at\n   `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx:77-83`), **then** focus returns to\n   that same task's Edit button once the form closes \u2014 same reasoning as\n   Acceptance Criterion 6.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- No new ARIA labelling beyond what already exists \u2014 `aria-label`,\n  `aria-live`, `role=\"status\"`, `role=\"alert\"`, and `role=\"group\"` are\n  already present throughout (e.g. `TaskList.jsx:89,108,117,124`,\n  `TaskFilter.jsx:9`, `App.jsx:65`) and are not part of this request.\n- No custom global keyboard shortcuts (e.g. a hotkey to jump to \"add task\"\n  or to a specific filter). The request asks for standard Tab/Enter/Space\n  operability, not new shortcuts.\n- No \"skip to content\" link. The app is a single short view (heading, one\n  form, one toolbar, one list) with no repeated navigation to skip past.\n- No change to visual design beyond the focus indicator itself \u2014 colours,\n  spacing, and layout are otherwise untouched.\n- No change to disabled-control behaviour (e.g. the disabled Add/Save/Clear-\n  completed buttons). Disabled controls are equally unreachable by mouse and\n  keyboard today, so they're not a \"mouse-only\" gap.\n- No backend changes \u2014 this is entirely a frontend concern.\n\n## Affected areas\n\n**Frontend only**\n- `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx` \u2014 add `autoFocus` to the task-title\n  input (AC1); accept a `ref`/imperative handle (or the App-level focus call\n  targets this input directly) so focus can be returned to it from\n  `App.jsx` when the list empties out (AC4).\n- `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx` \u2014 the `TaskRow` component needs:\n  after Delete/Done removes a row, move focus per AC4/AC5 (likely via a\n  callback the row calls just before/after invoking `onDelete`/`onComplete`,\n  since the row itself is about to unmount and can't move focus once it's\n  gone); after Escape/Cancel/Save closes edit mode, return focus to the\n  Edit button (AC6/AC7) \u2014 the Edit button already exists at\n  `TaskList.jsx:134`, needs a stable ref to focus.\n- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 owns the task array and knows sibling order, so\n  it's the natural place to compute \"which row/control gets focus next\" for\n  AC4/AC5 (TaskRow alone doesn't know about neighbouring rows) and to expose\n  a way to focus the AddTask input for the \"list is now empty\" and initial-\n  load cases.\n- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 add an explicit `:focus-visible` rule (or rules)\n  giving every interactive element a guaranteed-visible outline, including\n  extra contrast handling for `.filter-option.active` and\n  `.priority-select.priority-high`/`.priority-select.priority-low`.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- **Empty list on load:** AC1 still applies \u2014 focus goes to the AddTask\n  title input regardless of whether there are zero, one, or many tasks.\n- **Deleting the only remaining task:** falls through AC4's last case \u2014\n  focus goes to the AddTask title input, not `<body>`.\n- **Deleting the first or last row in a multi-row list:** AC4 covers both\n  directions (next, falling back to previous).\n- **Completing a task while filter is \"All\" or \"Done\":** row stays visible,\n  only the Done button disappears from that row \u2014 no focus-loss scenario\n  since the row (and its Delete button) is still there; see AC5's second\n  case.\n- **Completing a task while filter is \"Open\":** row disappears \u2014 same\n  handling as delete (AC5's first case).\n- **Search narrows the list to exclude the focused row's task:** out of\n  scope for this spec. Typing in the search input keeps focus in the search\n  input itself (the row disappearing doesn't strand focus, since focus\n  never left the search box) \u2014 no new handling needed.\n- **Escape during edit with an empty draft (validation error showing):**\n  Escape still cancels and discards the draft per the existing `cancel()`\n  behaviour (`TaskList.jsx:54-58`); AC6 still applies \u2014 focus returns to\n  Edit.\n- **Re-sorted list after a priority change moves a row's position:** React\n  reconciles by `task.id` (`TaskList.jsx:30`, stable `key`), so the DOM node\n  and its focus survive the reorder automatically \u2014 already correct, no\n  change needed, called out here so the reviewer knows it's a verified\n  no-op rather than an overlooked case.\n- **Focus-visible contrast on every button colour variant:** plain white\n  buttons, the dark `.filter-option.active`, and both `.priority-select`\n  colour variants all need to be checked individually (AC2), not just the\n  default case.\n\n## Open questions\n\n1. **Where exactly should initial focus land \u2014 the AddTask title input, or\n   somewhere else (e.g. the search box, or the first task row)?** Proceeding\n   under: the AddTask title input (AC1). Reasoning: the stated goal is\n   working through the list \"without touching the mouse,\" and creating a\n   task is the one action available even when the list is empty, making it\n   the only universally-sensible default. If the intent was instead \"land\n   on the list itself so I can immediately act on existing tasks,\" that's a\n   one-line change to what receives `autoFocus`.\n2. **Should Tab order be changed from DOM order (e.g. to visually reflect\n   priority sort order), or is DOM order acceptable?** Proceeding under: DOM\n   order is acceptable and unchanged \u2014 the existing markup order already\n   matches the visual layout top-to-bottom, left-to-right, which is the\n   expected/unsurprising tab order; nothing in the request asks for a\n   different traversal order, only that everything be reachable.\n\n## Note for the reviewer\n\n`CLAUDE.md` documents `cd frontend && npm test` as the frontend test\ncommand, but `frontend/package.json` has no `test` script and there are no\nexisting frontend test files at all (`find frontend/src -iname '*.test.*'`\nreturns nothing) \u2014 this predates this cycle and isn't caused by this\nfeature. The reviewer will need to either stand up a minimal frontend test\nsetup to write keyboard/focus tests, or verify this feature by driving the\nrunning app directly (e.g. via the `playwright` MCP tools already available\nin this environment). Flagging so it isn't mistaken for a regression caused\nby this change.\n"}, "session_id": "dc5f8c4c-3ccf-4cb5-b01c-d37b1d5c560b"}}
