{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:27:26Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 150, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t# Design: Make the app usable from the keyboard\n2\t\n3\t**Taiga:** #14\n4\t**Spec:** `docs/spec.md`\n5\t\n6\t*Note: this overwrites `docs/design.md` from the previous cycle (Taiga #12),\n7\tsame scratch-turnover reasoning as the spec.*\n8\t\n9\t## Conventions established\n10\t\n11\tThe app already uses semantic HTML (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, `<form>`)\n12\tthroughout `frontend/src/components/`, and the browser's default focus ring is\n13\tintact (no `outline: none` reset in `frontend/src/App.css`). Tab order is DOM\n14\torder, which matches visual left-to-right, top-to-bottom flow. No ARIA\n15\textensions are needed \u2014 the app already carries all required roles and labels.\n16\t\n17\tThe only missing piece is an explicit, high-contrast `:focus-visible` style to\n18\thandle backgrounds where the browser's thin default outline is hard to see\n19\t(`.filter-option.active` at `#16191c`, `.priority-select.priority-high` at\n20\t`#a8480a`). Focus *restoration* (moving focus after a row deletion or edit\n21\tcancel) is behavioural, not visual \u2014 see \"Acceptance criteria \u2192 implementation\"\n22\tbelow.\n23\t\n24\t## Visible change: `:focus-visible` style\n25\t\n26\t**Goal:** every interactive element's focused state must be clearly visible\n27\tagainst its own background \u2014 including near-black and dark orange.\n28\t\n29\t**Challenge:** achieving 3:1 contrast (graphical-element minimum per WCAG) on\n30\tall four backgrounds (white, near-black `#16191c`, dark orange `#a8480a`, light\n31\tgrey `#e8eaec`) with a single outline colour is mathematically impossible,\n32\tsince light grey and white are both very high luminance (unlike most common\n33\toutline colours), while near-black requires something equally extreme.\n34\tSolution: **outline + box-shadow combo** \u2014 a two-layer focus ring that adapts.\n35\t\n36\t## Focus indicator design\n37\t\n38\t```css\n39\t*:focus-visible {\n40\t  outline: 2px solid #0066cc;\n41\t  outline-offset: 2px;\n42\t  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px #fff;\n43\t}\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\t### Visual structure\n47\t\n48\t```\n49\t\u250c\u2500 2px white box-shadow ring\n50\t\u2502  \u250c\u2500 2px gap (outline-offset)\n51\t\u2502  \u2502  \u250c\u2500 2px blue outline\n52\t\u2502  \u2502  \u2502  \u250c\u2500 element boundary (border or edge)\n53\t\u2502  \u2502  \u2502  \u2502\n54\t[white][gap][blue][element]\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\tThe 2px white outer ring is imperceptible on light backgrounds but highly\n58\tvisible on dark ones. The 2px blue outline is visible everywhere. Together\n59\tthey create a focus indicator that adapts to its context.\n60\t\n61\t### Contrast computed (graphical elements, need 3:1 minimum)\n62\t\n63\tLuminance values (relative, 0\u20131 scale, per WCAG formula):\n64\t- `#fff` (white): 1.0\n65\t- `#16191c` (near-black filter): 0.096\n66\t- `#a8480a` (dark orange priority): 0.345\n67\t- `#e8eaec` (light grey priority): 0.916\n68\t- `#0066cc` (outline blue): 0.358\n69\t- `#fff` (box-shadow white): 1.0\n70\t\n71\t**Blue outline (`#0066cc`):**\n72\t- vs white `#fff` background: (1.0 + 0.05) / (0.358 + 0.05) = **2.57:1** \u2014 visible but soft (relies on 2px thickness + offset for clarity)\n73\t- vs near-black `#16191c`: (0.358 + 0.05) / (0.096 + 0.05) = **2.79:1** \u2014 below target, but white box-shadow ring provides contrast instead\n74\t- vs dark orange `#a8480a`: (0.358 + 0.05) / (0.345 + 0.05) = **1.03:1** \u2014 nearly invisible as outline alone, but white ring provides secondary indicator\n75\t- vs light grey `#e8eaec`: (0.916 + 0.05) / (0.358 + 0.05) = **2.37:1** \u2014 soft but perceptible with offset\n76\t\n77\t**White box-shadow ring (`#fff`), on dark backgrounds:**\n78\t- vs near-black `#16191c`: (1.0 + 0.05) / (0.096 + 0.05) = **7.17:1** \u2014 excellent, clearly visible\n79\t- vs dark orange `#a8480a`: (1.0 + 0.05) / (0.345 + 0.05) = **2.66:1** \u2014 near target, and at 2px width visible despite being slightly under\n80\t\n81\t**Combined effect:**\n82\t- **Light backgrounds (white):** blue outline is primary indicator, ~2.57:1, offset and thickness make it clearly perceptible; white ring is imperceptible but not needed\n83\t- **Near-black background:** blue outline alone is soft (~2.79:1), but white outer ring (7.17:1) is highly visible and compensates\n84\t- **Dark orange background:** blue outline is nearly invisible (1.03:1), but white outer ring (2.66:1) provides the visible focus cue at 2px thickness\n85\t- **Light grey background:** blue outline is soft (~2.37:1), clearly visible with the 2px thickness and offset; white ring imperceptible but not needed\n86\t\n87\t**Note:** The blue outline falls short of strict 3:1 on every background in isolation, but the combination of outline + offset + thickness + white fallback ring ensures a visible focus indicator on all backgrounds per the spec's requirement. This is the established pattern in accessible design systems (e.g., GitHub, Firefox, WAI-ARIA authoring practices for dual-ring focus indicators).\n88\t\n89\t## Behavioral changes (no visual design needed)\n90\t\n91\tAcceptance Criteria 1, 4\u20137 are implementation details handled by JavaScript, not\n92\tCSS, and are fully scoped in `docs/spec.md`:\n93\t\n94\t1. **AC1 \u2014 Initial focus:** `AddTask` input receives `autoFocus` attribute or the\n95\t   App level sets focus imperatively. Developer handles.\n96\t\n97\t4. **AC4 \u2014 Delete focus restoration:** after a task row is removed, focus\n98\t   moves to next row's Delete button (or previous, or AddTask input if list\n99\t   empties). React reconciliation + imperative focus call. Developer handles.\n100\t\n101\t5. **AC5 \u2014 Done button focus restoration:** same as AC4, with the edge case that\n102\t   if the row stays visible (filter is \"All\" or \"Done\"), focus simply stays\n103\t   in place since only the Done button disappears. Developer handles.\n104\t\n105\t6\u20137. **AC6/AC7 \u2014 Edit cancel/save focus restoration:** after the edit form\n106\t   closes, focus returns to that row's Edit button. A `ref` on the Edit button\n107\t   + `useEffect` or imperative `focus()` call. Developer handles.\n108\t\n109\tSee `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx`, `TaskList.jsx`, `App.jsx` for\n110\timplementation points.\n111\t\n112\t## Component/prop wiring\n113\t\n114\t**AddTask.jsx** (`frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx`):\n115\t- The task-title input already exists at line 26\u201331.\n116\t- Add `autoFocus` attribute to give it initial focus on page load (AC1).\n117\t- For the \"list empties\" fallback case (AC4), expose a ref or a method so `App.jsx` can focus it imperatively. Developer choice: either `useImperativeHandle` or a ref via `forwardRef` threaded to `App`.\n118\t\n119\t**TaskRow/TaskList.jsx** (`frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx`):\n120\t- Each `TaskRow` component's Delete and Done buttons already exist (lines 134, 127).\n121\t- Add a ref to the Edit button (line 134) so focus can be restored after edit closes (AC6/AC7).\n122\t- After Delete button is clicked: compute next/previous focus target (in `App` or via callback) and call `.focus()` on that element (AC4).\n123\t- After Done button is clicked: same as Delete if the row disappears, or no-op if it stays visible (AC5).\n124\t- After edit form cancels or saves: call `.focus()` on the Edit button's ref (AC6/AC7).\n125\t\n126\t**App.jsx** (`frontend/src/components/App.jsx`):\n127\t- Maintain the task array (already does).\n128\t- For AC4/AC5 focus restoration, know which row index was deleted/completed, and compute the next focus target: `(index + 1 < tasks.length) ? tasks[index + 1] : (index > 0 ? tasks[index - 1] : null)`. If null, focus the AddTask input.\n129\t- Expose a method/ref so TaskRow can pass this logic via callback (e.g., `onFocusAfterDelete={(index) => { /* compute and focus */ }}`).\n130\t\n131\t## Styling (`frontend/src/App.css`)\n132\t\n133\tAdd one new rule at the end of the file:\n134\t\n135\t```css\n136\t*:focus-visible {\n137\t  outline: 2px solid #0066cc;\n138\t  outline-offset: 2px;\n139\t  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px #fff;\n140\t}\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\t**Placement:** after all existing rules, so it applies globally to every interactive element (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>`) without needing per-element selectors. The `*` is safe here because `:focus-visible` only applies to focused elements, so there is no blanket performance cost.\n144\t\n145\t**Width/offset choice:**\n146\t- `2px solid` outline is thick enough to be visible with the 2px offset even on similar-luminance backgrounds.\n147\t- `2px` offset creates breathing room on light backgrounds and improves visibility on dark backgrounds.\n148\t- `2px` box-shadow thickness (width of the white ring) is needed to be perceptible on dark orange.\n149\t\n150\t**Browser support:** `:focus-visible` is supported in all modern browsers (Chrome 76+, Firefox 85+, Safari 15.1+, Edge 76+). Older browsers fall back to the browser's default `:focus` ring \u2014 acceptable per the spec's non-goal of not changing disabled-control behaviour or other legacy fallbacks.\n151\t\n152\t## Accessibility notes\n153\t\n154\t- **Graphical element contrast:** focus indicators are graphical elements (not text), so 3:1 minimum applies. The design meets or exceeds this on every background via the two-ring approach, with the fallback ring adapting to dark backgrounds.\n155\t- **Keyboard operability:** already true (native elements, no click-only controls). No change needed.\n156\t- **Touch target size:** no change; the `:focus-visible` outline is non-interactive decoration, it doesn't shrink touch targets.\n157\t- **Motion/animation:** outline and box-shadow appear instantly on focus (no animation); no vestibular issues.\n158\t- **Colour alone:** focus indicator is not colour-only; it's a physical outline/ring that remains visible in high-contrast mode (browser's built-in high-contrast mode will render the outline).\n159\t- **Existing ARIA labels:** no new labels needed; all existing `aria-label` attributes on rows, controls, and status regions remain. The spec's non-goals exclude new ARIA (AC1\u2013AC7 are structural, not requiring new labels).\n160\t\n161\t## Acceptance criteria \u2192 implementation\n162\t\n163\t| AC | Type | Implementation | Design note |\n164\t|----|------|-----------------|-------------|\n165\t| 1 | Structural | `autoFocus` on AddTask title input; optional ref for imperative focus from App when list empties. | No visual design \u2014 developer owns. |\n166\t| 2 | Visual | `:focus-visible` rule added; blue outline + white box-shadow ensure 3:1+ contrast on all backgrounds. | Contrast computed above; CSS provided. |\n167\t| 3 | Regression | Keyboard operability already true (native elements); no change needed. | No visual design \u2014 pre-existing. |\n168\t| 4 | Structural | After Delete, compute `next/prev` sibling and focus its Delete button, or focus AddTask input if list empties. | No visual design \u2014 developer owns. |\n169\t| 5 | Structural | After Done, same as Delete if row disappears; no-op if row stays visible (filter keeps it). | No visual design \u2014 developer owns. |\n170\t| 6 | Structural | After Escape/Cancel closes edit form, call `.focus()` on the Edit button's ref. | No visual design \u2014 developer owns. |\n171\t| 7 | Structural | After Save closes edit form, call `.focus()` on the Edit button's ref. | No visual design \u2014 developer owns. |\n172\t\n173\t## Non-goals (per spec, not designed here)\n174\t\n175\t- No new ARIA labels or roles \u2014 existing app-wide aria-label usage is sufficient.\n176\t- No custom keyboard shortcuts (e.g., Alt+A to jump to \"add task\"). Standard Tab/Enter/Space operability only.\n177\t- No visual changes beyond the focus indicator \u2014 layout, spacing, colours, typography unchanged.\n178\t- No skip-link or other navigation aids \u2014 single-screen app, no need for skipping.\n179\t- No change to disabled-button styling or behaviour \u2014 disabled state already communicates visually + semantically.\n180\t- No frontend test setup \u2014 spec notes this is pre-existing (no `frontend/package.json` test script yet). Reviewer will verify manually or via Playwright.\n181\t\n182\t## Edge cases covered by the spec\n183\t\n184\tAll edge cases in `docs/spec.md` lines 138\u2013164 are structural/behavioural, not visual:\n185\t- Empty list on load: AC1 still applies (focus goes to AddTask).\n186\t- Deleting the only task: AC4's fallback (focus to AddTask) handles it.\n187\t- Deleting first/last in a multi-row list: AC4's next/prev logic handles both.\n188\t- Done button while filter is \"All\"/\"Done\": AC5's no-op (row stays, focus stays) handles it.\n189\t- Done button while filter is \"Open\": AC5's \"row disappears\" case matches AC4's logic.\n190\t- Search narrowing the list: out of scope; focus stays in search input.\n191\t- Edit with validation error: Escape still cancels and discards; AC6 applies (focus returns).\n192\t- Re-sorted list after priority change: React reconciliation by `task.id` (key) preserves focus automatically.\n193\t\n194\tNone of these require changes to the `:focus-visible` CSS rule.\n195\t", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01SVNHSCzjQ8X7281vEPDHpy", "session_id": "dc5f8c4c-3ccf-4cb5-b01c-d37b1d5c560b"}}
