{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:38:05Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 440, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Agent", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_014EN7WBhauk8Dj3tVaAX5Cn", "input": {"description": "Test and review keyboard usability feature", "subagent_type": "reviewer", "run_in_background": false, "prompt": "Test and review the implementation of Taiga #14 \"Make the app usable from the keyboard\" in `/home/dev/projects/test-project`, branch `feature/tp-14/make-app-usable-from-keyboard`. Read, in order: `docs/spec.md` (7 acceptance criteria + edge cases + non-goals), `docs/design.md` (the focus-visible CSS design), and `docs/implementation.md` (what was actually built, including one documented deviation from the design). Then run `git diff -- frontend/` to see the actual code changes across `frontend/src/App.jsx`, `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx`, `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx`, `frontend/src/App.css`.\n\nImportant environment fact: **there is no frontend test runner or test files in this project at all** (`frontend/package.json` has no `test` script). This predates this cycle. Since this feature is about keyboard focus behavior, which can't be meaningfully verified by reading code alone, you'll need to stand up a minimal test setup to actually exercise it \u2014 e.g. `vitest` + `@testing-library/react` + `jsdom`, installed as devDependencies, is the standard fit for a Vite + React 19 project and lets you simulate keyboard events (`fireEvent.keyDown`, `userEvent.tab()`, etc.) and assert on `document.activeElement`. Neither you nor the developer have browser/Playwright tooling available in this session (that was tried and blocked by sandboxing), so jsdom-based component tests are the realistic verification path here \u2014 write real tests, run them, and report actual pass/fail, not a read-through.\n\nWhat to actually test against the 7 ACs in `docs/spec.md`:\n1. AC1 \u2014 on mount, the AddTask title input (`frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx`) has focus.\n2. AC2 \u2014 can't be verified in jsdom (no real layout/paint), so treat as a static code check: confirm the CSS rules in `frontend/src/App.css` (end of file) exist and target the right selectors. Not a blocker if this ends up being a \"read, don't test\" item \u2014 just say so plainly rather than fabricating a visual pass.\n3. AC3 \u2014 keyboard operability is a regression check; native elements already do this, low risk, but worth one quick userEvent-based sanity test if convenient.\n4. AC4 \u2014 deleting a middle row's Delete button moves focus to the next row's Delete button; deleting the last remaining row moves focus to the AddTask input. This is the most important behavioral test in this feature \u2014 the logic lives in `computeFocusFallback`/`handleDelete` in `frontend/src/App.jsx`.\n5. AC5 \u2014 completing a row's Done button under filter \"open\" moves focus per the same fallback logic; under filter \"all\" the row stays and no special handling applies (this is a documented spec decision, not a bug \u2014 see `docs/implementation.md`'s \"Other notes\" section).\n6. AC6/AC7 \u2014 closing a row's edit form via Escape, Cancel, or Save returns focus to that row's Edit button (`editButtonRef` + the mount-guard in `TaskList.jsx`'s `TaskRow`).\n\nAlso independently sanity-check the one documented deviation: in `docs/implementation.md`, the developer replaced the design's outset white `box-shadow` ring with an inset white `outline` for `.filter-option.active` and `.priority-select.priority-high` only, reasoning that an outset box-shadow paints over the surrounding page/container background rather than the element's own fill, and that flush-adjacent `.filter-option` siblings could occlude it. Check that reasoning holds and that the CSS in `frontend/src/App.css` matches what's documented. If you have any way to actually render/screenshot in your environment, use it \u2014 if not, say so honestly rather than claiming a visual check you didn't do.\n\nAlso try `cd frontend && npm run lint` (oxlint) yourself \u2014 it was blocked by sandboxing for both the developer and the orchestrator in their sessions; if it's also blocked for you, just note that plainly as an environment limitation rather than treating it as a finding either way.\n\nWrite your combined test+review verdict to `docs/test-review.md` following this project's template (approve, or changes-requested with concrete file/line pointers back to the developer). If everything passes, approve. If something is actually broken, changes-requested with exact repro and pointers \u2014 do not rubber-stamp."}, "session_id": "dc5f8c4c-3ccf-4cb5-b01c-d37b1d5c560b"}}
